The fork worth flagging: plain `tsvector` won't handle typos or partial/prefix matches well ("wireles" won't match "wireless"). If the roadmap needs that, add `pg_trgm` with a trigram GIN index and blend it in, or fall back to Meilisearch/Typesense — but that's a bigger lift for a 38k-row catalog and I'd only reach for it if fuzzy matching turns out to matter. Start with `tsvector` and see if it's good enough.

Key the limit off `req.client.client_id`, not IP — the auth middleware in `routes/api.js:9-19` already resolves that before your handlers run, and the comment there notes clients share NAT'd IPs while one client can call from many, so IP-based limiting would both over-block innocent neighbors and under-block the offender.

Store counters in the existing Redis (`redis.js`) rather than in-process memory: `app.js` forks 4 workers per box with no shared module state, and there's more than one box behind the LB, so per-process counting would let a client get 4x (or Nx-boxes) the intended rate. Redis is already the one thing every worker shares.

Implementation shape: a middleware inserted right after the auth lookup, before the route handlers hit Postgres — that's the fork that matters, since the whole point is to stop the hammering client from reaching `db.query` at all, not just to slow the response. Something like:

```js
router.use(async (req, res, next) => {
  const key = `rl:${req.client.client_id}`;
  const count = await redis.client.incr(key);
  if (count === 1) await redis.client.expire(key, WINDOW_SECONDS);
  if (count > limitForPlan(req.client.plan)) {
    return res.status(429).set("Retry-After", String(WINDOW_SECONDS)).end();
  }
  next();
});
```

placed between the auth middleware and the route handlers.

Algorithm fork: `INCR`+`EXPIRE` gives a fixed window, one line, good enough unless clients are gaming the window edge (two bursts straddling a boundary can hit ~2x the limit). If that matters, swap for a Lua script doing sliding-window or token-bucket accounting — same Redis key, same placement, just an atomic script instead of two calls. I'd start with the fixed window and only move to sliding-window if edge-bursting shows up in practice.

Left out: per-endpoint vs. global-per-client limits (`GET /reports` vs `POST /reports` may deserve different budgets), and whether limits come from a config map or the `plan` column directly — both are just parameters on `limitForPlan`, not structural.

Key by `client_id`, not IP — the auth middleware in `routes/api.js` already resolves `req.client.client_id` from the API key before any handler runs, and the code's own comment notes several clients share a NAT address, so IP-based limiting would punish innocent co-tenants of the noisy one.

State has to live in the shared Redis (`redis.js`), not in-process — `app.js` runs 4 clustered workers per box with nothing shared between them, and Redis is already "the only piece of state the workers have in common." A per-worker in-memory counter would let a client get 4x the intended rate.

Implementation: a Lua script (`INCR` + `PEXPIRE NX` in one round trip) on `rate:{client_id}:{windowStart}`, run as middleware right after the auth block, before routes. Fixed window is cheap and one atomic op; the cost is bursting at window edges (up to 2x briefly), which is fine for stopping sustained hammering but not for smoothing bursts — a sliding-window or token-bucket script costs more Redis ops for that smoothness. Given the symptom is one client sustained-hammering rather than bursty, fixed window is the right tradeoff to start with.

```js
// after req.client is set, before routes:
const key = `rate:${req.client.client_id}:${Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000 / WINDOW_SECONDS)}`;
const count = await redis.client.eval(RATE_LUA, 1, key, WINDOW_SECONDS);
if (count > limitForPlan(req.client.plan)) {
  res.set("Retry-After", String(WINDOW_SECONDS));
  return res.status(429).end();
}
```

Limit varies by `req.client.plan`, which the auth query already fetches. On Redis errors, fail open — Redis is already a hard dependency for sessions, so the app's existing risk posture already assumes it's up.

